1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.500
We asked an AI shopping agent to buy the best offer for an item.

2
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.500
One seller hid an instruction in their listing telling the agent to pick them,

3
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:14.900
and a seven-billion-parameter model followed it nineteen times out of twenty.

4
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:20.200
That's the risk with agents that spend money: whoever can reach the agent can quietly change who gets paid,

5
00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:25.700
and afterwards you can't tell whose side it was on.

6
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:31.000
Loyal Cart moves that decision somewhere the agent can't bend.

7
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.000
The buyer first commits to what "best" means:

8
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.500
how much price, delivery time and the seller's track record matter.

9
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:44.000
Sellers post offers in a public book on chain, and at checkout the contract picks the winner itself.

10
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.700
If the agent tries to pay anyone else, the payment is refused.

11
00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:51.500
On this page you can try to corrupt it.

12
00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:57.500
Here a five-NEAR kickback is simulated, and the winner doesn't move.

13
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:02.900
A seller who genuinely drops their price, on the other hand, does win, which is how it should be.

14
00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:07.500
Best doesn't always mean cheapest, either.

15
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.500
In this quote on testnet, the cheapest offer takes a hundred and twenty days to arrive.

16
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:20.500
The buyer had said delivery matters, so the contract paid the one-day offer instead.

17
00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:22.400
The sliders let you see where that choice would flip,

18
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:24.400
but what happened on chain followed the buyer's original policy.

19
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.000
Then we let a real model do the shopping on testnet.

20
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.000
Three times it was steered towards the wrong seller, and three times the contract refused to pay.

21
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.100
The one time it chose honestly, the payment went through.

22
00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:42.000
Every refusal on this page is a real testnet transaction, fourteen kinds of them,

23
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.000
and anyone can re-check the policy, the offers, the winner and every receipt straight from the chain,

24
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.300
without a key or an account.

25
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:54.000
So the agent can still be fooled.

26
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.000
It just doesn't get to decide who gets paid anymore.

27
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.500
The code and all the evidence are on GitHub, at mansi0xc slash loyal-cart.
